# HD Format Specification

This document is the human-readable specification of a valid `.hd` file. The agent-facing version with the same rules lives at `.claude/skills/hd-format/SKILL.md`.

## File structure

1.  **Optional YAML frontmatter** at the top of the file, fenced with `---` on its own line. No HTML comment wrapper.
    
2.  **One blank line** after the frontmatter is a convention for readability, not a requirement.
    
3.  **The body** follows. In format version 2 it is GFM Markdown with raw-HTML islands; in legacy version 1 it is body-only HTML. Either way the file must not contain `<html>`, `<head>`, or `<body>` tags.
    

Minimal example:

```
---
title: Getting Started
id: <22 chars of base62>
format-version: 2
---

# Getting Started

Welcome.
```

## Frontmatter fields

All fields are optional. The editor will inject `id` and `format-version` automatically on first open if they are absent.

| Field | Type | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `id` | base62 string (22 chars) | Per-document identifier. Stable across renames and moves; asset folders are keyed by it. Do not edit by hand — clear it to force regeneration. |
| `format-version` | integer | Version of the **HD file format** the document is stored in. Current: **2**. A missing value means "the latest version." **Not a document revision number — never increment it.** See [The `format-version` field](#the-format-version-field). |
| `title` | string | Human-readable title. |
| `date` | ISO 8601 date or datetime | Document date. |
| `author` | string | Author identifier (email or handle). |
| `description` | string | One-line description. |
| `tags` | list of strings | Tags / categories. |
| `purpose` | string | Optional free-text statement of what the document is for. |

### The `format-version` field

> [!IMPORTANT]
> `format-version` records **which version of the HD file format the document is stored in**. It is not a revision counter, a draft number, or a changelog. It describes the *container*, not the *content* — so editing a document does not change it.
>
> **The only correct value for authored content is `2`, and it must never be incremented.** Writing `3`, or bumping it "to keep it current", declares a format that does not exist and breaks the file. The number moves only when the HD format itself changes, which is a change to the editor, not to any document.

**Renamed from `version`.** The field was originally called `version`. That name was routinely misread as the document's own revision number and incremented, silently reclassifying valid v2 files. `format-version` cannot be misread that way, which is the entire reason for the rename.

Both names are **read**, with a fixed precedence:

1. `format-version` — canonical. If present, it wins outright.
2. `version` — deprecated fallback, for documents written before the rename. Consulted only when `format-version` is absent.

If a document carries both, `format-version` is authoritative and the stale `version` is ignored — never merged, never treated as a conflict.

Only `format-version` is **written**. The editor auto-corrects on the first edit of any document: it writes `format-version: 2` and **removes the legacy `version` key entirely**, so an inconsistent document repairs itself as soon as it is edited. Opening a document never rewrites its version fields.

A document is treated as legacy version 1 **only when it explicitly declares format version `1`** under either key. Anything else — including a document with no version field at all — is read as current version 2.

To record a document's own revision, add a separate field of your own (`revision`, `doc-version`, …). Do not repurpose `format-version`.

## Supported elements

### Block

-   `h1` through `h6`
    
-   `p`, `blockquote`, `pre`, `hr`, `div`
    

### Lists

-   `ul`, `ol`, `li`
    
-   `dl`, `dt`, `dd`
    

### Tables

-   `table`, `caption`
    
-   `thead`, `tbody`, `tfoot`
    
-   `tr`, `th`, `td`
    
-   `colgroup`, `col`
    

### Inline text

-   `a`
    
-   `strong`, `em`, `b`, `i`, `u`, `s`
    
-   `code`, `kbd`, `samp`, `var`
    
-   `mark`, `sub`, `sup`
    
-   `abbr`, `cite`, `q`, `small`
    
-   `span`, `br`
    

### Media

-   `img`, `figure`, `figcaption`
    
-   `svg` and its standard children for inline diagrams
    

### Sectioning

-   `section`, `article`, `aside`
    
-   `header`, `footer`, `nav`, `main`
    

### Disclosure

-   `details`, `summary`
    

## Excluded elements

-   `html`, `head`, `body` — body-only rule.
    
-   `script`, `style` — no executable code or stylesheets.
    
-   `iframe`, `embed`, `object` — no embedded external content.
    
-   `form`, `input`, `button`, `textarea`, `select`, `label`, `fieldset` — no interactive controls.
    
-   `canvas`, `audio`, `video` — no programmatic graphics or media playback.
    
-   `meta`, `link`, `base`, `title` — head-only elements.
    

## Supported attributes

### Universal (allowed on any element)

-   `id`, `class`
    
-   `title`
    
-   `lang`, `dir`
    
-   `style` — inline only; the `<style>` tag itself is excluded
    
-   `data-*`
    

### Element-specific

-   `a`: `href`, `target`, `rel`, `download`
    
-   `img`: `src`, `alt`, `width`, `height`, `loading`
    
-   `td`, `th`: `colspan`, `rowspan`, `scope`, `headers`
    
-   `ol`: `start`, `type`, `reversed`
    
-   `details`: `open`
    
-   `col`, `colgroup`: `span`
    
-   `code`, `pre`: `class` for syntax-language hints (e.g., `language-typescript`)
    

## Excluded attributes

-   Event handlers: `onclick`, `onload`, and any `on*` attribute.
    
-   Form-control attributes.
    
-   Interactivity attributes: `contenteditable`, `draggable`, `hidden`.
    

## Markdown round-trip behavior

HD's **Copy as Markdown** command converts the document to Markdown. For each allowed element, the table below records whether the conversion is lossless, lossy, or falls back to raw HTML embedded in the Markdown output.

### Status legend

-   **Lossless** — Markdown has a native syntax that preserves all relevant attributes.
    
-   **Lossy** — Markdown can represent the element but loses some attributes (e.g., colspan, inline styles, classes).
    
-   **Raw HTML** — Markdown has no equivalent; the element is emitted as raw HTML inside the Markdown output.
    

### Element matrix

| Element | MD Status | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `h1`–`h6` | Lossless | ATX headings (`#`...`######`). |
| `p` | Lossless | Plain paragraph. |
| `strong`, `b` | Lossless | Both map to `**…**`; the strong/b distinction is lost. |
| `em`, `i` | Lossless | Both map to `*…*`; the em/i distinction is lost. |
| `u` | Raw HTML | Markdown has no underline syntax. |
| `s` | Raw HTML | GFM has `~~…~~` for strikethrough; turndown emits raw HTML by default. |
| `code` (inline) | Lossless | Backticks. |
| `pre` + `code` | Lossless | Fenced code block. `class="language-…"` is preserved as the info string. |
| `blockquote` | Lossless | Lines prefixed with `>`. |
| `blockquote[data-callout]` | Lossless | A GitHub-style alert: `> [!NOTE]` (also `TIP`, `IMPORTANT`, `WARNING`, `CAUTION`) alone on the first line. An unrecognized type stays an ordinary blockquote. |
| `ul`, `ol`, `li` | Lossless | Bulleted/numbered lists. `ol`'s `start` attribute is supported in GFM. |
| `dl`, `dt`, `dd` | Raw HTML | No standard Markdown for definition lists. |
| `a` | Lossless | `[text](href)`. `target` and `rel` are dropped. |
| `img` | Lossy | `![alt](src)`. `width`, `height`, `loading` are dropped. |
| `hr` | Lossless | `---`. |
| `br` | Lossless | Backslash line break or trailing spaces. |
| `table` (simple) | Lossy | GFM table syntax. No colspan/rowspan, no multi-paragraph cells, no inline styles. |
| `table` (complex) | Raw HTML | Tables with colspan/rowspan, merged cells, or block content in cells are emitted as HTML. |
| `thead`, `tbody`, `tfoot`, `tr`, `th`, `td`, `caption`, `colgroup`, `col` | Lossy / Raw | Follow the `table` ruling: lossy when the table is GFM-representable, raw otherwise. |
| `div`, `span` | Raw HTML | No Markdown equivalent. |
| `section`, `article`, `aside`, `header`, `footer`, `nav`, `main` | Raw HTML | Sectioning elements have no Markdown equivalent. |
| `figure`, `figcaption` | Raw HTML | Markdown has no figure/caption construct. |
| `mark` | Raw HTML | No standard Markdown for highlight. |
| `sub`, `sup` | Raw HTML | No standard Markdown. |
| `kbd`, `samp`, `var`, `abbr`, `cite`, `q`, `small` | Raw HTML | No Markdown equivalents. |
| `details`, `summary` | Raw HTML | Renders inline in some Markdown flavors; turndown emits as HTML. |
| `svg` | Raw HTML | Inline SVG passes through unchanged. |

### Attributes always dropped when converting to Markdown

-   Universal: `id`, `class`, `title`, `lang`, `dir`, `style`, `data-*`
    
-   Anchor: `target`, `rel`, `download`
    
-   Image: `width`, `height`, `loading`
    
-   Table cell: `scope`, `headers`
    
-   Ordered list: `type`, `reversed` (only `start` survives in GFM)
    
-   `details`: `open`
